  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{A}{(3x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3x+2)(2x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =B(\\frac{9+4}{2}) \\\\ B & =\\frac{26}{13} \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{-2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =A(\\frac{-13}{3}) \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA+B=-3+2=-1.\n$$",
